{"chapter": 4, "sha256": "415dcaf9d8e7cdb4e7eb3ef006ada5c28967dbec74ea4039fe62897c0fde142f", "voice": {"voice_id": "ppLqTilh7rH7fbUVlXsf", "name": "David - Documentary", "model_id": "eleven_multilingual_v2", "voice_settings": {"stability": 0.55, "similarity_boost": 0.8, "style": 0.15, "use_speaker_boost": true}}, "source": "docs/script.md", "files": {"narration": {"file": "ch4.mp3", "text": "Egypt did not think it began with its first king. A papyrus in Turin, copied about 1250 BCE, lists the kings back to Menes, and before him gods, then spirits, then the Followers of Horus. In the most probable reading of its damaged numbers, it gives the spirits and Followers 13,420 years and, in the next line, 23,200. Manetho, a thousand years later, tells the same story.\n\nThe Egyptians called the beginning sep tepi, the First Time. Hancock and Bauval read it as a memory, not a myth: a golden age, they write, that immediately followed creation.\n\nHere is the plateau as they picture it. The lion and its two temples stand alone. Where the pyramids will rise, nothing stands yet.\n\nA stela found beside the Great Pyramid says Khufu found a house of Isis \"beside the house of the Sphinx\". If that is true, they argue, the Sphinx was there before Khafre.\n\nOn the walls of King Unas's burial chambers at Saqqara, carved about 2400 BCE, the oldest copy of the Pyramid Texts sends the king into the sky. \"Unas appears as a star.\" \"Orion is encircled by the Duat,\" it says, and so are Sothis, the star we call Sirius, and Unas.\n\nNone of these texts gives a date. Hancock and Bauval look for one in the sky.", "seconds": 94.74, "alignment": {"characters": ["E", "g", "y", "p", "t", " ", "d", "i", "d", " ", "n", "o", "t", " ", "t", "h", "i", "n", "k", " ", "i", "t", " ", "b", "e", "g", "a", "n", " ", "w", "i", "t", "h", " ", "i", "t", "s", " ", "f", "i", "r", "s", "t", " ", "k", "i", "n", "g", ".", " ", "A", " ", "p", "a", "p", "y", "r", "u", "s", " ", "i", "n", " ", "T", "u", "r", "i", "